{
  "purpose": "Kyrgyz term glossary. Handed to the content pipeline with every ky translation request (doc/i18n.md), so the same word is used for the same thing in every event. Kyrgyz is the language with the fewest sources and the one Claude is weakest in, so this file exists before the pipeline does.",
  "updated": "2026-09-06",
  "confidence": {
    "high": "Everyday Kyrgyz or a borrowing already settled in Kyrgyz school books. Use as written.",
    "check": "Claude's best guess. A Kyrgyz teacher has to confirm it before any event using it leaves 'machine' status."
  },
  "rules": [
    "Scientific terms mostly arrive through Russian and keep the Russian form (физика, энергия, орбита). Do not invent a native calque for them.",
    "Where an everyday Kyrgyz word already means the thing, prefer it over the borrowing: күч, жарык, сан, кан, оору.",
    "If neither is certain, use the Russian borrowing and leave the translation for review rather than coining a word.",
    "Kyrgyz is subject-object-verb. Translating a Russian or English sentence clause by clause produces text nobody speaks; rebuild the sentence.",
    "Years and numbers are never translated - the site formats them (formatYear.ts): б.з.ч. 585, б.з. 499, болжол менен, 1830-жылдар, V кылым.",
    "Markdown structure survives translation untouched: figure paths and credits, $...$ maths, and the box keywords ([!NOTE], [!TIP], [!WARNING]) stay in English while their contents become Kyrgyz.",
    "Voice: warm but respectful, second person plural (сиз). No exclamation marks, no 'amazing'."
  ],
